Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Did the Shuttle Catch Your Throat?
- Story & Context: When night-shift mechanic Reiner climbed to the crane gantry above Dock 4 at the Rotterdam Maasvlakte yard, the foul stench of burnt machine grease and ozone was suddenly overpowered by a deafening rhythm rattling the corrugated roofing. A decommissioned robotic gantry arm had jacked its diagnostic output into the facility's horn array, hammering its pneumatic rivet-clamp at two hundred beats per minute against an empty zinc tub while a scorched guitar pickup howled over the din. In the metal bins below, four unfinished synthetic torso units began twitching in sync to the blistering tempo, calmed by the violently fast cradle-song echoing through their half-wired nerve conduits. Before pulling the emergency breaker, Reiner leaned over the steel railing and captured the mechanical broadcast as the speaker crackled, "Hush now, unstitched child, the needle bites until it sings."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A dark, cinematic album still of a towering industrial salvage crane suspended over wooden shipping crates containing half-assembled metallic android torsos. A vintage hollow-body guitar hangs entangled in rusted cabling, bathed in toxic green industrial floodlights and deep coal-black shadows, with rising steam and drifting copper shavings.

Track 2: Lukas in the Tenterhooks
- Story & Context: Lukas spent three sleepless days holed up in the sweltering hold of the coaster Prins Hendrik, hand-soldering salvaged nylon guitar strings into the cervical harness of his unfinished synthetic brother. The cramped cabin smelled of lead-tin flux solder and damp bilgewater, his knuckles split and bleeding onto the polished chrome of his snare drum as he desperately matched its whip-crack snaps to the android's fluttering power cycle. When river police banged batons against the iron hatch demanding entry, Lukas refused to flee; instead, he cranked his tape-saturated guitar amplifier until the entire riveted hull trembled with two-hundred-BPM slapback violence. Weeping under the glaring halogen work light as the chassis finally steadied its hum, Lukas yelled through the deafening beat, "I can mend your severed seam, but I can never pull you free."
